            <span>
              Ancient & Early Medieval
            </span>

            <h3>
              Foundations on the Chotanagpur plateau
            </h3>

            <p>
              Archaeological finds indicate settlements across
              the Chotanagpur plateau. The region's forests and
              mineral-rich land nurtured distinct indigenous cultures.
            </p>

          </article>


          <article class="jt-about-history-item">

            <span>
              Medieval Era
            </span>

            <h3>
              Chieftaincies, forts and trade routes
            </h3>

            <p>
              Local chieftaincies and forts emerged; trade routes
              linked forests and plains. Sacred sites grew around
              rivers and hills.
            </p>

          </article>


          <article class="jt-about-history-item">

            <span>
              Colonial Period
            </span>

            <h3>
              Resistance and community movements
            </h3>

            <p>
              Tribal resistance against exploitative systems marked
              the era — including significant movements led by
              community leaders across the plateau.
            </p>

          </article>


          <article class="jt-about-history-item">

            <span>
              Statehood Movement
            </span>

            <h3>
              A separate regional identity
            </h3>

            <p>
              20th-century social and cultural mobilizations
              advocated a separate identity for the “land of forests.”
            </p>

          </article>


          <article class="jt-about-history-item">

            <span>
              15 November 2000
            </span>

            <h3>
              State formation
            </h3>

            <p>
              Jharkhand became India's 28th state, with Ranchi
              as the capital — catalyzing focused development of
              tourism, heritage, and ecology.
            </p>

          </article>

